Picture a pot of water boiling on the stove. We know—from chemistry—that water boils at 100°C at sea level. Now say you stick a thermometer in and it reads 99.8°C. Pretty close, right? That thermometer is accurate. But if another one reads 115°C, something's off—that one's way off the mark, not accurate at all.

So accuracy is really just about being *right*. It's asking: how close did you actually get to the real answer? {{viz:anim-01}}

Here's the key idea to hold onto: somewhere out there is a TRUE value—100°C for boiling water, say. Your measurement either lands near it (accurate) or misses it by a mile (inaccurate). Think of it like your dart throw: bullseye = accurate, way off in the corner = inaccurate.

So accuracy answers one simple question: "Did I measure the right value?"

Here's the catch though—you can only judge accuracy if you already know the true value. If nobody's ever measured the real answer, how would you know whether your reading is close or not? No true value to compare against means no way to call something accurate or inaccurate. {{viz:chart-01}}
